M etal−organic frameworks (MOFs) are a class of hybrid linkers.1−3 In many instances coordinatively unsaturated metal sites (either at the linkers or the nodes) are essential for generating desired functional behavior such as catalysis,4 gas storage,5 and gas separation.6 Most often MOFs have been metal-functionalized from the condensed phase (i.e., solution) either in de novo fashion or via postsynthesis modification. Unfortunately, considerable effort is necessary to ensure that excess metals or undesired solvent and other reagents are removed from solution-metalated MOFs. Solvent molecules can also irreversibly ligate otherwise coordinatively unsaturated metal sites, thus yielding less than desirable materials properties.8 Hence, new synthetic strategies capable of uniformly incorporating open metal sites within MOFs may be attractive for obtaining new functional materials.
Postsynthesis metalation from the vapor-phase could eliminate purification and activation steps and circumvent problems associated with site blocking by solvent molecules. Meilikhov et al. synthesized a variety of metal inclusion compounds (i.e., “metal@MOF” host−guest complexes) by using chemical vapor infiltration from volatile metal complexes under “sublimation-like” conditions. Ideally, this infiltration strategy can lead to high metal@MOF loadings and hybrid materials such as MOFs containing nanoparticles.9 Unfortunately, there is little control over the spatial distribution of metal species within the MOF and in some instances host− guest interactions may lead to unstable metal@MOFs.
Atomic layer deposition (ALD) is a vapor-phase synthetic technique traditionally used for depositing thin films.10 The key conceptual advance that ALD embodies over other techniques (e.g., chemical vapor deposition/infiltration) is that precursor molecules deposit only at chemically reactive surface sites, and these reactions are self-limiting. A well-known example makes use of trimethylaluminum $\left(\mathrm{AlMe}_{3}\right)$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ to construct thinfilms of $\mathrm{Al}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3},$ Scheme 1a.11 Given the sequential and selflimiting nature of ALD, it is uniquely suited for conformal coating of porous materials that exhibit ultrahigh-aspect ratios.12,13 To date, some striking examples encompass deposition onto micro- and nanopowders14 and coating of nanoparticle films15 as well as aerogel networks.16 ALD is also capable of depositing metal complexes if the sequential coreactant (e.g., ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{\cdot}^{\cdot}$ ) is not used during the deposition. Given the aforementioned capabilities of ALD, we wondered if metalation by ALD In a MOF (AIM, Scheme 1b) could be accomplished; i.e. could MOFs serve as platforms for selflimiting/spatially oriented, interior/surface reaction chemistry?
Three design criteria were considered en route to a MOF broadly suitable for AIM. First, we reasoned that mesoporous (rather than microporous) channels would be necessary to facilitate the diffusion of ALD reactants within the MOF.13 Recall that diffusion times scale inversely with channel width squared (and directly with channel length squared).17,18 Second, the desired MOF must be thermally and hydrolytically stable; many ALD reactions are facile between $100{-}300\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and employ steam as a coreactant.11 Finally, spatially oriented functional groups (e.g., $-\mathrm{OH}$ groups) must be present for selflimiting metalation reactions to ensue. Given these stringent criteria, we targeted a MOF constructed from hexa-ZrIV nodes19 and tetratopic linkers.20,21 Such MOFs are stable to $500~^{\circ}\mathrm{C},^{19}$ can contain mesoporous channels,20 and (in principle) can contain spatially oriented $-\mathrm{OH}$ groups.21,22 We describe the synthesis and characterization of a $Z\mathbf{r}$ -based MOF (NU-1000, vide inf ra) that satisfies all of the aforementioned criteria. We then used NU-1000 as a platform to demonstrate quantitative, self-limiting metalation by AIM.
Solvothermal reactions of $\mathrm{zrCl}_{4},$ $^{1,3,6,8}$ -tetrakis( $p$ -benzoicacid)pyrene $\left(\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{TBAPy}\right),^{23,24}$ and benzoic acid in diethylformamide (DEF) yielded crystals suitable for single-crystal X-ray analysis. The parent-framework node consists of an octahedral $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ cluster capped by eight $\mu_{3}{\mathrm{-OH}}$ ligands. Eight of the twelve octahedral edges are connected to TBAPy units, while the remaining $\mathrm{Zr}$ coordination sites (after activation) are occupied by eight terminal $-\mathrm{OH}$ ligands.22 The 3D structure can be described as 2D Kagome sheets linked by TBAPy ligands. Four of the eight terminal $-\mathrm{OH}$ groups point into the mesoporous channels, while the remaining terminal hydroxyls lie in smaller apertures between the Kagome sheets. The resultant MOF (Figure 1) has the molecular formula $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}(\mu_{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{OH})_{8}(\mathrm{OH})_{8}.$ - $\mathrm{TBAPy})_{2}$ (NU-1000).25,26 Finally, it was necessary to “activate” NU-1000 with a $\mathrm{HCl}/N,N.$ -dimethylformamide (DMF) mixture in a procedure similar to that of Feng et al.21 $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ NMR and diffuse reflectance infrared Fourier transform spectroscopies reveal that HCl removes benzoic acid from the $\mathbf{Zr}_{6}$ nodes, leaving behind terminal $-\mathrm{OH}$ groups (SI).
We initiated AIM (vide inf ra) with a microcrystalline powder of NU-1000, reasoning that this form would facilitate diffusion of ALD precursors within the MOF. The microcrystalline powder was obtained by switching to DMF as a synthesis solvent. Powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) patterns of the DEF (simulated)- and DMF-prepared samples are identical, and Pawley refinement of the DMF pattern (SI) shows that the unit cell and symmetry are identical also. In the following, we discuss DMF-prepared samples only.
The $\Nu_{2}$ adsorption isotherm of NU-1000 is best described as type IVc (Figure 3); NU-1000 has a Brunauer−Emmett−Teller (BET) surface area of $2320~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and a total pore volume of $1.4\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . The experimentally measured surface area and total pore volumes are in excellent agreement with the theoretical values of $2280\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and $1.4~\mathrm{{cm}^{3}~g^{-1}}$ obtained from grand canonical Monte Carlo simulations (GCMC) and subsequent BET analysis (Table S3, SI). DFT-analyzed pore-size distributions indicate pores of diameters at ${\sim}12$ Å (consistent with the triangular micropores) and 30 Å (hexagonal mesopores) of NU-1000. Thermal gravimetric analysis (TGA) of a sample activated at $120~^{\circ}\dot{\mathrm{{C}}}$ demonstrated that NU-1000 is stable up to $500^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The presence of $-\mathrm{OH}$ groups was confirmed by diffuse reflectance infrared Fourier transform spectroscopy (DRIFTS). Sharp peaks appear at wavenumbers 3674 and $\bar{36}55~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (black curve in Figure 4), which we have assigned to the terminal and bridging $-\mathrm{OH}$ stretches of the $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\big(\mu_{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{OH}\big)_{8}(\mathrm{OH})_{8}$ node. These sharp peaks are similar to those measured previously for bridging $-\mathrm{OH}$ stretches on the $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ node of UiO- $66^{27,28}$ and for bridging and terminal $-\mathrm{OH}$ stretches in bulk $\mathrm{Zr(O)}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ powders.29 TGA and temperature-dependent DRIFTS data (SI) are also quantitatively consistent with the assigned $-\mathrm{OH}$ content of NU-1000. In addition, the temperature-dependent DRIFTS data show that the $-\mathrm{OH}$ functionality persists under the conditions of our ALD experiments (vide inf ra).
Microcrystalline samples of NU-1000 were placed in an ALD reactor at 140 or $110^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and exposed to diethylzinc $\left(\mathrm{ZnEt}_{2}\right)$ or $\mathrm{AlMe}_{3}$ . The ALD reactions were carried out with the following timing sequence (in seconds): $t_{1}\mathrm{~-~}t_{2}\mathrm{~-~}t_{3},$ where $t_{1}$ is the precursor pulse time, $t_{2}$ the precursor exposure time, and $t_{3}$ the $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ purge time. Metalation was confirmed via inductively coupled plasma-optical emission spectroscopy (ICP-OES). The resultant materials have been termed ${\bf{Z}}{\bf{n}}$ -AIM and Al-AIM. On average, we observe $0.5~\mathrm{Zn}$ or $1.4\mathrm{Al}$ atoms per $\mathrm{Zr}$ atom, Table 1. These values correspond to three $Z\mathbf{n}$ or eight Al atoms for every $\mathbf{Zr}_{6}$ node in NU-1000. Consistent with ALD-like behavior, longer exposure times (via repeated precursor exposure) did not lead to greater metal loading. To ascertain whether we were fully accessing NU-1000 under our ALD conditions, we extended exposure times to several hours, rather than seconds, by immersing samples in solutions containing $\mathrm{ZnEt}_{2}$ or $\mathrm{AlMe}_{3}$ (Table 1, Zn-NU-1000 and Al-NU-1000). The loadings obtained through extended solution-phase exposure are only slightly higher than those from transient ALD.
PXRD measurements (Figure 2) showed that both ${\bf{Z}}{\bf{n}}$ -AIM and Al-AIM retain their crystallinity. BET analyses of the $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ adsorption isotherms (Figure 3) indicate a decrease in surface area from $2230~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ for NU-1000 to 1580 and $1160~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ respectively for ${\bf{Z}}{\bf{n}}$ -AIM and Al-AIM. The gravimetric and volumetric (SI) surface areas (along with total pore volumes) decrease with increasing metal loading (Table 1). DFT poresize distributions (Figure 3), extracted from the $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ isotherms, indicate that the average diameter of the mesopore shifts from ${\sim}30\mathrm{~\AA~}$ in NU-1000 to ${\sim}27\$ Å for both ${\bf{Z}}{\bf{n}}$ -AIM and Al-AIM.
DRIFTS measurements (Figure 4) confirmed that metalation occurs by reaction with $-\mathrm{OH}$ groups. Clearly, the sharp peaks at 3674 and $3655~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ have been significantly or completely reduced. For ${\bf{Z}}{\bf{n}}$ -AIM, we speculate that the metalation changes the local symmetry about the $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ cluster, shifting the frequencies of the terminal $-\mathrm{OH}$ stretches. Additional studies are in progress to be reported in due course. The DRIFTS data suggest that $\mathrm{ZnEt}_{2}$ is able to react only with $-\mathrm{OH}$ groups pointing into the hexagonal channels, while $\mathrm{AlMe}_{3}$ reacts with all terminal $-\mathrm{OH}$ groups. (Our findings are reminiscent of the observations of Larabi et al. who quantitatively titrated with a solution reagent the $-\mathrm{OH}$ ligands on the $\mathrm{Zr_{6}(O)_{4}(O H)_{4}}$ node of UiO-67.28) To assess $Z\mathrm{n}$ and Al incorporation by individual MOF microcrystallites, we turned to scanning electron microscopy−energy dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (SEMEDX). As shown in Figure 5a, only $\mathrm{Zr}$ (black trace) was detected in NU-1000; Zn (purple trace) and Al (red line) do not rise above the baseline. The blue line in the image indicates where the EDX scan was performed. When scanning $\mathbf{Zn-}$ and Al-AIM (Figure ${\mathfrak{s b}},\mathbf{c}$ ), $Z\mathrm{n}$ (purple line) and Al (red line) were detected in the entire NU-1000 crystal.
As a proof-of-concept to show that AIM could be used to elicit new functional behavior we turned to chemical catalysis. The Knoevenagel condensation between ethyl cyanoacetate and benzaldehyde can be catalyzed by Lewis acids, albeit in limited scope and conversion rate.30 As anticipated, the ${\boldsymbol{Z}}\mathbf{r}^{\mathrm{{IV}}}$ sites in NU-1000 proved inactive toward the Knoevenagel condensation; however, Zn-AIM and Al-AIM were active catalysts, which we attribute to the presence of Lewis acidic $\mathsf{A l}^{\mathrm{III}}$ and $\mathrm{\dot{Z}n^{I I}}$ sites in ${\bf{Z}}{\bf{n}}{\bf{-\nabla}}$ and Al-AIM.31 After catalysis, the solutions were filtered to remove the MOF and were examined by ICP-OES; no $Z\mathbf{n}$ or Al was found, consistent with the assignment of Znand Al-AIM as the catalysts. The results demonstrate the potential for ALD to incorporate spatially oriented single-sitelike catalytic moieties into ultrahigh-aspect ratio MOFs.
In conclusion, we synthesized and characterized a new $Z\mathbf{r}.$ - based MOF, NU-1000. Given its thermal stability, large 1D pores and apertures, and spatially isolated −OH groups (on the $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ nodes), NU-1000 is an excellent platform for metalation by AIM and that ${\bf{Z}}{\bf{n}}$ -AIM and Al-AIM can be readily synthesized. Based on repetitive precursor/water exposure cycles, initial follow-up studies (to be described in detail when complete) have revealed that AIM (with NU-1000) can be used to incorporate (a) multiple other metals, (b) a binary combination of metals, and (c) sizable clusters of metal oxides. We anticipate that AIM will be useful for generating or enhancing MOF chemical competency for a wide variety of applications, including catalysis (demonstrated herein), sorption, and separations.
